<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah [Laws of the Afternoon Prayer]<br>Halachah 7, Part 4 (§§ 56–81)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:49</span> (<i>LaShemesh Ohel Bahem</i>), continued and concluded</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 56 — R' Shimon bar Yochai: the Zohar as Tikun of the Contractions; Lag BaOmer</div>

<div class="p">The essential tikun through R' Shimon bar Yochai and his companions, who said: <span class="mx">"there [among the previous students] fear was fitting; here we exist through love."</span> R' Shimon engaged especially in repairing the contractions — tikun of vessels to draw the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> and reveal His G-dliness. The entire Zohar HaKadosh — especially the Idra Rabba and Idra Zuta — drew and repaired wondrous contractions (= <span class="h">metzach</span>, <span class="h">aynin</span>, <span class="h">tikunai dikna kadisha</span> [forehead, eyes, tikun of the holy beard]) — all awesome contractions through which the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> is perceived. Therefore he said: <span class="mx">"through this [the Zohar] they will leave exile"</span> — through tikun of the contractions = essential tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = redemption (<span class="mx">"the matter depends only on <i>teshuvah</i>"</span> [Zohar]). He guaranteed: the Torah will not be forgotten from Israel — <span class="v">"it shall not be forgotten from the mouth of his seed."</span></div>

<div class="p">R' Shimon departed on Lag BaOmer. In Eretz Yisrael, great joy is made at his <span class="h">hilula</span> — seemingly one should mourn! But <span class="h">minhag Yisrael Torah</span> [the custom of Israel is Torah]: the joy is that such a <span class="h">tzadik</span> existed, who revealed such awesome Torah and made such tikun — through which the Torah will never be forgotten. He completed this specifically through his departure (repairing the flaw of R' Akiva's students who died prematurely). He revealed the Idra Zuta at his departure — besides the Idra Rabba, Sifra d'Tzniusa, and the entire Zohar in his lifetime. Through all this: in every generation, whoever wishes to return can draw close from any place, through the tikun of the contractions he made in his life and especially on his holy day of departure (<span class="mx">"the <i>tzadikim</i> are greater in death than in life"</span>).</div>

<div class="p">Now, the Torah's sustenance and our entire hope is through the great departed <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (<span class="mx">"were it not for the prayers of the dead <i>tzadikim</i> for the living, the world would not endure a single moment"</span> [Zohar]). R' Shimon and his companions were the tikun of R' Akiva's students who died (= death of the <span class="h">Melachim</span> = Breaking of Vessels). Through the departed students' souls, wondrous tikun was made — since their lights already existed and were withdrawn, a <span class="h">reshimu</span> remained, enabling even greater tikun (= tikun of the Breaking). [See the Idra Rabba (135a): <span class="mx">"were they abolished? Rather, they were withdrawn from that tikun until the tikun of Adam came, and then all were restored."</span>]</div>

<div class="p">Four entered the <span class="h">Pardes</span>: [Ben Azzai] did not contract the light → gazed and was stricken (= damaged the song simple). [Ben Zoma] tried to contract but saw the tremendous turmoil of <span class="h">yetzirah l'vish</span> and was terrified → gazed and died (= damaged the song double). [Acher] entered the battle of the inclinations and did not withstand → cut the saplings (= damaged the song triple) — <span class="mx">"Greek song never ceased from his mouth"</span>; when he left the study hall, heretical books fell — he fell into external wisdoms through not nullifying his pride before the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. R' Akiva entered in peace and departed in peace — facing tremendous tests (the wife of Turnus Rufus, etc.) — he wept, laughed, and spat. His soul departed with the <span class="h">Dales</span> of <span class="v">"Echad"</span> — where the entirety of song simple/double/triple/quadruple is encompassed. He merited that all tikun should be through him. But his students flawed through <span class="h">machaloikes</span> — dying between Pesach and Shavuos. The tikun began through R' Shimon bar Yochai.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 57 — The Splitting of the Sea: <i>Mochin d'Katnus</i>; <i>Tefilah l'Ani</i> Reaches Even There</div>

<div class="p">The splitting of the <span class="h">Yam Suf</span>: the Ari"zal explains it is the secret of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> [extremely constricted consciousness] that can only be sweetened through <span class="h">Atika</span>: <span class="v">"why do you cry to Me? The matter depends on <i>Atika</i>."</span> Through what was explained above (§§40-41): one can understand wondrous hints for strengthening in prayer from any place — even from the most extreme <span class="h">katnus</span> (alluded to in the danger at the sea [the Ari"zal's <span class="h">kavanos</span> regarding the serpent]). Still — <span class="mx">"there is no despair in the world"</span>: there is hope through <span class="h">Atika</span> — from the secret of <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span> of barley = the <span class="h">asiris ha'aifah</span> not revealed to Avraham. One must hold on [<span class="h">der haltin zich</span>] even there — through a part from among millions and myriads that one still has (= the poor man's offering that can come in halves and portions). Prayer even in this state can save — such prayer is exceedingly precious (<span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span>, the <span class="h">asiris ha'aifah</span>). The secret of how far His mercy extends = <span class="v">"why do you cry to Me? — the matter depends on <i>Atika</i>"</span> = <span class="h">Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</span> where Yosef/Moshe/David departed (§46).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"Your wife is short — bend down and whisper to her"</span> (= prayer). When <span class="h">Malchus</span> is in <span class="h">katnus</span> (= the lesser luminary), one must raise it through prayer even in the utmost <span class="h">katnus</span> and lowliness — raising both luminaries. <span class="mx">"Whisper"</span> = the silent prayer. Sometimes one is in such <span class="h">katnus</span> that one cannot raise one's voice at all — yet one must not despair of prayer: <span class="v">"brought low, you shall speak from the earth... from the dust your speech shall chirp."</span> = <span class="v">"from the depths I called You, Hashem."</span> = <span class="v">"their heart cried to Hashem."</span> This is the silent <span class="h">Shemoneh Esrai</span> established for the smallest of the small and the greatest of the great. The small — their entire prayer is whispered prayer. Their tikun: through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who pray silently, ascending to <span class="h">Atika</span> where there is <span class="h">lachash</span> [whisper] (= <span class="mx">"a wise <i>charashim</i> [artisan] and skilled in <i>lachash</i> [whisper]"</span>). There: <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence"</span> — this secret cannot be spoken openly. <span class="v">"Hashem will fight for you, and you shall be silent"</span> — cry in whisper from the heart's depths. First: silent prayer; then, through the <span class="h">tzadik</span> raising the prayers: prayer in a loud voice.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 58 — "Let the Wicked Abandon His Way": Hashem's Mercy Beyond Comprehension</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Let the wicked abandon his way, the man of iniquity his thoughts — let him return to Hashem, who will have mercy, and to our G-d, who abundantly pardons."</span> The essential: abandon the wicked way henceforth; the bad thoughts that are the essential evil inclination — and do not despair saying there is no hope! <span class="v">"Return to Hashem, who will have mercy... who abundantly pardons"</span> = the secret of <span class="h">Minchah</span> — Hashem adds one forgiveness of His own (§42). <span class="v">"My thoughts are not your thoughts... My ways are higher than your ways"</span> — it is impossible to understand how far His mercy extends. His mercy never ends.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 59 — The Splitting of the Sea on the Seventh of Pesach: Through the <i>Omer</i></div>

<div class="p">The splitting of the <span class="h">Yam Suf</span> on the seventh of Pesach — after Israel brought the <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span> and counted six days. The miracle was through <span class="h">Sefiras HaOmer</span> (six days already counted [see <span class="h">kavanos</span>]). The splitting = <span class="h">Minchah</span> = tikun of the <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> from which all sins derive. Through the <span class="h">asiris ha'aifah</span> (= the poor man's offering in utter destitution) = the deep secret not revealed to Avraham. In these latter days, Hashem reveals this through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — from there the secret of the splitting: <span class="mx">"the matter depends on <i>Atika</i>."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 60 — The Song at the Sea: <i>Shir Pashut</i>; <i>Tzitzis</i></div>

<div class="p">The Song at the Sea derives from the Song of the Future (= <span class="h">shir pashut</span> etc.) — through tikun of the contractions (prayer/<span class="h">teshuvah</span>). = <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span>. The Song concludes: <span class="v">"the Sanctuary, Hashem, Your hands established — Hashem shall reign forever"</span> — the essential tikun = revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> through <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> (= the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>). This verse is doubled — through <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>'s ascent, <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascends too. The Midrash: the miracle was in the merit of <span class="h">tzitzis</span> (= <span class="h">shir pashut kaful</span> etc., as explained in the teaching).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 61 — Yisro Heard and Converted: <i>Malchus</i> Revealed Through Contraction and Battle</div>

<div class="p">Yisro: <span class="mx">"what news did he hear and come? — the splitting of the sea and the war with Amalek."</span> The splitting = contraction of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>. The sea's excess waters = excess <span class="h">da'as</span> (<span class="v">"the earth shall be full of knowledge of Hashem as waters cover the sea"</span>) — one cannot pass through excessive <span class="h">da'as</span>. Hashem split the sea = contraction to the sides. But immediately the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> awakened (= bad thoughts = Amalek: <span class="v">"he chanced upon you"</span>). Therefore the splitting is adjacent to the war with Amalek: after contracting, one must overpower bad thoughts. Through this: <span class="h">Malchus</span> revealed to all — even converts come and convert (Yisro = the most distant recognizing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 62 — Yosef's Merit: the Splitting in <i>Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</i></div>

<div class="p">The miracle of the splitting was through the merit of Yosef HaTzadik: <span class="v">"the sea saw and fled [<i>va'yanos</i>]."</span> Yosef was the first of the three <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who departed at Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span> in <span class="h">Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</span> (= <span class="h">Atika</span>), from where the miracle came. Therefore in <span class="h">Parshas B'Shalach</span> (the splitting): <span class="v">"Moshe took Yosef's bones with him"</span> — from there the miracle.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 63 — Gleanings: Yosef's Gathering; Rus; <i>Likkutim</i>; the <i>Tzadik</i> Gathers Every Good Point</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"When you reap your land's harvest, do not complete the corner of your field... leave gleanings for the poor and the stranger"</span> — adjacent to the <span class="h">Omer</span>, <span class="h">Sefirah</span>, and Shavuos. The essential <span class="h">Matan Torah</span> at Shavuos (drawn each year through Moshe = the generation's true <span class="h">tzadik</span>) is all through <span class="h">likkutim</span> [gleanings]. Yosef HaTzadik (= <span class="h">Mashiach</span>) sustained Israel in Egypt so they would not sink into its contamination — Moshe later redeemed them through Yosef's bones. The true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> glean holy sparks through every good point of every Jew: <span class="v">"Yosef gathered all the money."</span> The redemption depends on clarifying all holy sparks that fell through the Breaking of Vessels — only through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. The Adversary expands, especially against those wishing to draw close. But the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= <span class="v">"rejoices like a mighty man to run the course"</span>) — his power through each Jew leaving bad thoughts. He suffers greatly from us (<span class="v">"he was wounded for our transgressions"</span>). The essential: the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power — every good point snatched is immediately placed into the holy house he builds (= <span class="h">Mishkan</span>/<span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>). Even if one falls afterward — the arousal is not lost (already entered the building). <span class="mx">"Do not abandon your place"</span>; <span class="mx">"no good will is ever lost."</span> Even sinners of Israel are full of <span class="h">mitzvos</span> like a pomegranate. (As he warned: go with "<span class="h">Azamrah</span>" constantly.)</div>

<div class="p">Through these <span class="h">likkutim</span>, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> transmits Torah (= <span class="h">Shavuos</span>). We count the days to the <span class="h">Omer</span> (= the poor man's offering at the beginning of the harvest). The tikun through the <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span> = the poor man's offering: the <span class="h">tzadik</span> raises it through his great power, gleaning good points from utter destitution, building the holy house, drawing Torah. Therefore we read <span class="h">Rus</span> (who came at the barley harvest — from her came <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, the poor man riding on a donkey). David always called himself poor: <span class="v">"poor am I and dying since my youth"</span>; <span class="v">"poor and destitute am I."</span> <span class="v">"She went and gleaned in the field"</span> — all the tikun of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (= <span class="h">Mashiach</span>) is through <span class="h">likkutim</span>. Therefore the holy books are called <span class="h">Likkutim</span> — and Rabbainu called all his books <span class="h">Likkutim</span>. All Torah revealed by the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> is through these <span class="h">likkutim</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 64 — <i>Pai'ah</i> and <i>Leket</i>: Leaving for the Poor = for One's Own Poor Soul</div>

<div class="p">Leaving <span class="h">pai'ah</span> and <span class="h">leket</span> for the poor = <span class="h">tzedakah</span> = tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span>. Through this, power is added to the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> (poor in exile) and to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, called "poor") — to glean in the supernal field, gathering every good arousal, building the holy structure. Also alluded: when one engages in worldly business, eating and drinking (= "reaping" = the purpose of planting) — one must aim for the eternal purpose, earning reward in the supernal field: <span class="v">"he shall come with song, carrying his sheaves."</span> Happy are the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who receive nothing from this world. For the rest: the Torah warns — do not complete the reaping entirely for bodily desires! Leave <span class="h">pai'ah</span> and <span class="h">leket</span> for the poor (= give <span class="h">tzedakah</span>). Also hinted: leave <span class="h">pai'ah</span> and <span class="h">leket</span> for "the poor one" = one's own soul, impoverished in exile within — have mercy on one's soul, leaving something from one's appetites. Through this, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= the poor one) gathers all good points and builds the holy structures. Through these <span class="h">likkutim</span>: Torah is drawn (= <span class="h">Shavuos</span>). Therefore this verse is adjacent to the <span class="h">Omer</span> and Shavuos.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 65 — "If This Repulsive One Meets You — Drag Him to the <i>Bais Midrash</i>"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"If this repulsive one [the evil inclination] meets you — drag him to the <i>Bais Midrash</i>."</span> The essential tikun through the holy <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who expound and reveal wondrous Torah <span class="h">chiddushim</span> appropriate to the generation, person, place, and time — strengthening and restoring every soul, even those severely flawed. When one flees there: <span class="mx">"if it is stone, it melts; if iron, it shatters"</span> — <span class="v">"are My words not like fire... like a hammer shattering rock?"</span> The holy teachings of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> — all study halls receive from him — are like coals of fire, a hammer shattering rock: one verse splitting into many meanings for each person according to need. Through this: <span class="v">"I shall remove the heart of stone from your flesh."</span> The <span class="h">menuvval</span> [repulsive one] = the most loathsome and filthy level of the evil inclination. <span class="h">Paga</span> [met] = a harmful encounter (we pray: <span class="mx">"save us from harmful encounters"</span>). The essential tikun: drag one's thought into the holy <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> — remembering that one is part of its holy gathering.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 66 — "My Sins Overwhelmed Me — You Will Atone; Happy Is He Who Dwells in Your Courts"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Matters of sin overwhelmed me — our transgressions, You will atone. Happy is he whom You choose and bring near, to dwell in Your courts; we shall be satisfied with the goodness of Your house, the holiness of Your Temple."</span> Sins overwhelm — our only strength is in our mouths, praying: <span class="v">"our transgressions, You atone."</span> As the prior verse: <span class="v">"Hearer of prayer — to You all flesh shall come"</span> — even one as flawed as I can come through prayer. The essential strengthening: the Adversary tries to weaken even from this — praying and crying to Hashem. Against this: <span class="v">"happy is he who dwells in Your courts — satisfied with the goodness of Your house"</span> — the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (= <span class="h">mikdash m'at</span> [miniature sanctuary]). There is nowhere to flee except the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span>, joining the holy gathering of souls drawing close to the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= the <span class="h">Rosh Yeshivah</span>). Every good point snatched, every thought detached from bad to good, is immediately stored in this <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> (= <span class="h">Mishkan</span> built by the <span class="h">tzadik</span>). Adjacent: <span class="v">"with awesome deeds in <i>tzedek</i> You answer us — the confidence of all the ends of the earth and the distant seas"</span> — even the most distant, through the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span>'s power, have confidence and are answered wondrously.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 67 — "Happy Is the Man Whose Strength Is in You": the <i>Bais Midrash</i> as Eternal Refuge</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Happy is the man whose strength is in You"</span> — adjacent to <span class="v">"happy are those who dwell in Your house."</span> All strength and fortitude through being among "those who dwell in Your house." Through this: <span class="v">"they shall praise You, Selah"</span> — the eternal purpose: thanking and praising Hashem forever. The entire <span class="h">Mizmor</span> speaks of the greatness of Israel's <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span> (= <span class="h">Mishkan</span>/<span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>): <span class="v">"how beloved are Your dwelling places... my soul yearns for Hashem's courts."</span> <span class="v">"Even the sparrow finds a home"</span> — souls wandering far (<span class="v">"like a bird wandering from its nest"</span>) find a home through the <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span>. <span class="v">"The <i>d'ror</i> [free bird] finds a nest"</span> — even those who slip away from holiness constantly (like the <span class="h">tzippor d'ror</span> that escapes) find a nest to remain in Israel's holiness. <span class="v">"Where she placed her chicks — Your altars"</span> — the <span class="h">afro'chim</span> [fledglings] = fallen souls (Zohar: birds whose wings are not whole). Even they draw near through the "altars" — the exertions of the distant ones to learn and pray (= offerings: <span class="v">"for Your sake we are killed all day"</span>).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Passing through the valley of weeping, they make it a spring"</span> — through the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span>'s holiness, even those who fell into the valley of weeping (= <span class="h">Gehinom</span> [Rashi]) find the spring flowing from Hashem's house (= <span class="v">"a spring from Hashem's house shall water the valley of <i>Shittim</i>"</span> — to atone for the deeds of <span class="h">Shittim</span> [= the essential craving]). <span class="v">"Blessings shall clothe the guide"</span> — the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who teaches the way (Rashi: the one who admonishes and instructs). Even from the valley of weeping: blessings arise, which the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (head of the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span>) draws down as deep counsel even for them. <span class="v">"Look upon the face of Your <i>Mashiach</i>"</span> — every <span class="h">tzadik</span> is <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, building the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> through the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> gathering, drawing close the most distant — the essential redemption: <span class="v">"a redeemer shall come to Tziyon, to those who return from transgression."</span> <span class="v">"A day in Your courts is better than a thousand; I chose to stand at the threshold of my G-d's house rather than dwell in the tents of wickedness."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 68 — "I, Through Your Abundant Kindness, Shall Enter Your House"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"I, through Your abundant kindness, shall enter Your house."</span> David first said: <span class="v">"You destroy speakers of falsehood"</span> (= Do'eg and Achisofel, the opponents). Then: <span class="v">"I — through Your abundant kindness, shall enter Your house."</span> The Midrash: though "I did as they did" — yet through Hashem's kindness, "I shall enter Your house." Every person should say likewise: knowing the pain of one's sins — all one's vitality and sustenance is through not opposing the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, striving to draw close. One must enliven oneself with this: all my life-force is from being among those who dwell in the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>. <span class="v">"Through Your abundant kindness — I shall enter Your house."</span> The essential: the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> named after the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (our Sages [Sanhedrin 32a]: <span class="mx">"<i>Tzedek tzedek</i> — pursue justice: follow the sages to their academy — R' Eliezer to Lod, R' Yosei to Tzippori, Rebbi to Bais She'arim"</span> — in every generation).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 69 — "Hashem, You Have Been Our Dwelling in Every Generation": the <i>Bais Midrash</i> as Eternal Refuge</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Hashem, <i>ma'on</i> [dwelling] You have been for us in every generation."</span> <span class="h">Ma'on</span> = the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> = the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= <span class="h">mikdash m'at</span>). In every generation: a <span class="h">yeshivah</span> of students with a true master expounding true Torah <span class="h">chiddushim</span>. One must follow this <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> in every generation. The Torah is eternal — no generation is entirely orphaned — a <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> named after the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> exists even now. There one finds <span class="h">ma'on</span> — a dwelling to flee from the repulsive one who attacks constantly.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Before the mountains were born and You formed the earth... from eternity to eternity, You are G-d."</span> = Before Creation. <span class="v">"From world to world You are G-d"</span> = He is One before and after forever. All Creation through the above contractions. Therefore: <span class="v">"You return man to <i>dakah</i> [being crushed]... and say: return, children of man."</span> The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> through <span class="h">emunah</span> — knowing that from eternity to eternity He is G-d, all will ultimately be repaired, yet some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> is needed. Even one crushed to the crushing of soul — <span class="v">"return, children of man"</span> — there is always hope: <span class="v">"I, Hashem, have not changed; and you, house of Yaakov, have not been consumed."</span> <span class="v">"A thousand years in Your eyes are like yesterday passing"</span> — all that passes (ascents, descents, even over a thousand years like Adam HaRishon) is like one day passing, = the cycle of revelation and concealment = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. His good measure is greater: He contracts Himself daily from <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> to illuminate man's heart. Each day: the evil inclination renews, but Hashem helps. All depends on man each day — contracting, guarding from bad thoughts. Whoever holds to the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> and does not despair — always has hope — through the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> (= <span class="v">"<i>Ma'on</i> You have been for us"</span>). Through this: <span class="v">"You return man to <i>dakah</i>"</span> — even from the ultimate descent.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 70 — "To This One I Look: the Poor and Broken-Spirited"</div>

<div class="p">The essential: feeling one's poverty (= the <span class="h">Hai</span> was a <span class="h">Dales</span>) — then specifically it is restored to <span class="h">Hai</span>. <span class="v">"The heavens are My throne... what house will you build Me?... To this one I look: the poor and broken-spirited."</span> The essential building and sustenance of the holy house is through the poor and broken-spirited. = The <span class="h">Korban Minchah</span> (poor man's offering = <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span>). The <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s purpose = the offerings — and the choicest of all is the <span class="h">asiris ha'aifah</span> (not revealed to Avraham). Now: the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span>'s building and sustenance through <span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span> = <span class="mx">"whoever is small is great."</span> Through sensing one's lowliness for good (to draw close, not distance) — one is among those of the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> who received the wondrous Torah <span class="h">chiddushim</span> filled with counsel — through which one tikunim the contractions, receives <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in measure, guards from bad thoughts, reveals His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. David: <span class="v">"in my poverty I prepared for the house of my G-d"</span> — specifically through always considering himself poor (<span class="v">"I am poor and destitute"</span> — countless times in <span class="h">Tehillim</span>): through this he prepared the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 71 — <i>Tzitzis</i>: Gathering the Dispersed to the Holy Gathering</div>

<div class="p">All the above tikun through the holy gathering. Therefore: <span class="h">tzitzis</span> and <span class="h">t'chailes</span>. <span class="h">Tzitzis</span> gathers the dispersed — gathering the four <span class="h">tzitzis</span> at <span class="v">"bring us to peace from the four corners of the earth."</span> <span class="v">"Ten men shall take hold of the corner of a Jewish man's garment"</span> (our Sages: each Jew will have 2,800 servants through the four corners of the <span class="h">tallis</span>). <span class="h">Tzitzis</span> = tikun of the contractions through the holy gathering of the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> = the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. Therefore we request: <span class="v">"lead us swiftly upright to our land."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 72 — The Four Banners Around the <i>Mishkan</i>: Uniting All Israel</div>

<div class="p">The four banners [<span class="h">d'galim</span>] surrounding the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> (with the <span class="h">Aron</span> and the Ten Commandments) — traveling in four directions. = <span class="h">Tzitzis</span> on four corners (= four directions) = the <span class="h">Dales</span> of <span class="h">Echad</span> = the four letters of the Name = <span class="h">shir pashut kaful m'shulash m'ruba</span>. Tikun of the contractions requires all Jewish souls (encompassed in 600,000 letters of Torah) united together. The four banners with the Ten Commandments among them = the song and ten types of melody = <span class="h">tzitzis</span> = tikun of the contraction — receiving <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>'s light in measure for life, revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> to all = <span class="h">Shavuos</span> (called <span class="h">Atzeres</span> = <span class="h">Malchus</span>). Therefore <span class="h">Parshas Bamidbar</span> (= the four banners) before <span class="h">Shavuos</span> — uniting in love.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 73 — Three Daily Prayers; <i>Krias Shma</i> and <i>Tefilah</i>: <i>Baisa Ila'ah</i> and <i>Tata'ah</i></div>

<div class="p">Three daily prayers. <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> = <span class="h">yichuda ila'ah</span> = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>. <span class="h">Tefilah</span> = <span class="h">yichuda</span> and <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>. <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>'s <span class="h">kavanos</span> = unifying <span class="h">Abba</span> and <span class="h">Imma</span> (= <span class="h">Chochmah</span> and <span class="h">Binah</span>) to draw <span class="h">mochin</span> = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>. Through <span class="v">"Shma Yisrael"</span> we reveal His unity — drawing <span class="h">emunah</span> into our hearts. But <span class="h">Malchus</span> is not yet revealed to all. The contraction was for revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> — its completion when all accept it. In <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>, declaring His <span class="h">Malchus</span> in the <span class="h">Dales</span> of <span class="h">Echad</span> over six directions — all still in potential. Then: <span class="h">V'ahavta</span> (accepting <span class="h">Malchus</span>), <span class="h">V'haya Im Shamo'a</span> (accepting <span class="h">mitzvos</span>: <span class="v">"guard yourselves lest your heart be enticed"</span> — bad thoughts = idolatry). Then: <span class="h">Parshas Tzitzis</span> (= <span class="h">t'chailes</span> that consumes all — but Israel clinging to Hashem receive life). Then: <span class="h">Emes V'Yatziv</span> — drawing the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power (Moshe/<span class="h">Mashiach</span>: the first and final redeemer). The entire <span class="h">berachah</span> testifies: all is true and established — the Torah received from Moshe and the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> after him. It speaks of the Exodus and the splitting — through which <span class="h">Malchus</span> was revealed: <span class="v">"together they acknowledged and crowned Him: Hashem shall reign forever."</span> = <span class="v">"Moshe and the Children of Israel sang to You."</span> Then: <span class="h">Tefilah</span> begins — the essential revelation of <span class="h">Malchus</span>. Through prayer, Israel can alter nature — all recognize: <span class="v">"Hashem is G-d, ruling His world, doing the will of those who pray."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 74 — "<i>Baruch Shem K'vod Malchuso</i>" in a Whisper After <i>Shma</i></div>

<div class="p">Immediately after <span class="v">"Shma Yisrael"</span>: <span class="v">"<i>Baruch Shem K'vod Malchuso l'olam va'ed</i>"</span> in a whisper. This declares: through accepting His G-dliness (= <span class="h">Shma</span> = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>), we will merit revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> to all forever (even the most distant from holiness). Said in a whisper: we have not yet merited this fully — the matter must be hidden lest the Adversary be provoked. We still must battle to overpower good thoughts against bad — accomplished through <span class="h">V'ahavta</span>, <span class="h">V'haya</span>, <span class="h">Vayomer</span>, then <span class="h">Emes V'Yatziv</span> (the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power). But the essential revelation of <span class="h">Malchus</span> is through <span class="h">Tefilah</span>. (The <span class="h">kavanos</span>: in <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>, <span class="h">mochin</span> are drawn to build <span class="h">Malchus</span>; the essential building and unification is through <span class="h">Tefilah</span>.)</div>

<div class="sn">§ 75 — <i>Teshuvah</i> Through the <i>Tzadik</i>; "<i>Imma</i> Lends Her Garments to the Daughter"; <i>Yom Kippur</i></div>

<div class="p">The essential tikun through <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power (without him, we cannot raise hand or foot). But the <span class="h">tzadik</span> cannot complete his desire without us (some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> is essential). Whoever holds to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> — strengthening through everything as he warned: "however things are, strengthen yourself" — is always among the <span class="h">ba'alai teshuvah</span>; all descents transform to ascents, sins to merits. Even from the worst places — holding to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> — the <span class="h">tzadik</span> raises him, raising through him fallen sparks that could not be raised otherwise (= new vessels are formed).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Teshuvah</span> = <span class="mx">"<i>Imma</i> lends her garments to the daughter"</span>: <span class="h">Imma</span> = <span class="h">Binah</span> = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>; the daughter = <span class="h">Malchus</span> = <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> = <span class="h">tefilah</span>. <span class="h">Malchus</span> cannot overpower the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> and opponents alone — only through the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who overpowered the evil inclination completely, attained <span class="h">Malchus</span> in perfection, merited perfect prayer (= the weapon of war). <span class="mx">"A crier proclaims daily: whoever slays the serpent shall receive the king's daughter — which is prayer"</span> (Tikunai Zohar). Most of Israel — even the worthy — are far from this weapon (perfect prayer). Their power: only through this <span class="h">tzadik</span> who makes them <span class="h">ba'alai teshuvah</span> — receiving power to fight through <span class="h">Imma</span> lending garments to the daughter. Though one lacks weapons of one's own (not having stood in battle to overpower thoughts), through Hashem's mercy one receives power from <span class="h">Binah</span> (= <span class="h">Imma</span>) lending to the <span class="h">ba'alai teshuvah</span>'s prayer.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> = the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: <span class="mx">"<i>Imma</i> and daughter ascend together"</span> (Zohar). Hashem pardons through <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — even though one did not purify the qualities properly (which should be drawn from <span class="h">Binah</span> to <span class="h">Malchus</span>). In His mercy: <span class="h">Imma</span> and daughter ascend together.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 76 — <i>Yom Kippur</i>: Seven Times "Hashem Hu HaElokim"; <i>Shma</i> and <i>Baruch Shem</i> Aloud</div>

<div class="p">At <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>'s conclusion: <span class="v">"Hashem Hu HaElokim"</span> seven times; <span class="h">Shma</span> and <span class="h">Baruch Shem</span> aloud after <span class="h">Ne'ilah</span>. = <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> — both houses ascend = <span class="h">Shma</span> and <span class="h">Baruch Shem</span> now said aloud (no fear — sins are forgiven). Seven times <span class="v">"Hashem Hu HaElokim"</span> = the seven qualities that must be purified (§§10ff.). Though we failed — through <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>'s power (sins transformed to merits), it is as if we purified all seven qualities, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> to all.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 77 — Five Prayers of <i>Yom Kippur</i> = the Letter <i>Hai</i>; <i>Ne'ilah</i> = <i>Minchah</i></div>

<div class="p">On <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>: five prayers (unique in the entire year). Prayer = <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = the <span class="h">Hai</span>. Five prayers = the <span class="h">Hai</span>'s tikun in perfection. The added prayer: <span class="h">Ne'ilah</span> = also <span class="h">Minchah</span> time (our Sages). All tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = <span class="h">Minchah</span>. Therefore: two prayers at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time — uniting <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">tata'ah</span> (whose essential unity is through <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = <span class="h">Minchah</span>). After <span class="h">Ne'ilah</span>: <span class="h">Shma</span> and <span class="h">Baruch Shem</span> aloud; seven times <span class="v">"Hashem Hu HaElokim"</span> = His <span class="h">Malchus</span> revealed in completeness.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 78 — <i>Shavuos</i>: Reciting the Beginning and End of Each <i>Sidra</i> and <i>Masechta</i></div>

<div class="p">On <span class="h">Shavuos</span>: we recite the beginning and end of each <span class="h">sidra</span> and <span class="h">masechta</span>. The beginning = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>; the end = <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>. <span class="h">Baisa ila'ah</span>: contracting <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> to receive in measure — but not yet complete until one overpowers bad thoughts, serves in gradation, and reveals His <span class="h">Malchus</span> (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>). Then: <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> The beginning of each <span class="h">sidra</span>/holy book = the illumination of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> through Torah's contracted letters (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>); the end = the clarification complete (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> = <span class="h">Malchus</span> revealed).</div>

<div class="p">The entire Torah is in every person, every time, every place. Every day: <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> illuminates one's heart. Everything we learn — from <span class="h">Bereishis</span> to <span class="v">"before the eyes of all Israel"</span>, Nevi'im, Kesuvim, Shas — all is <span class="mx">"in order to know Him"</span> = drawing <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in measure through Torah's contracted letters. Now, after the forty-nine days of <span class="h">Sefirah</span> (= forty-nine gates of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>), purifying from contamination — we engage only in the beginning and end (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">tata'ah</span>). = <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> (= the fiftieth day = <span class="h">Yovel</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>): through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power, both houses ascend — even without having fully purified the qualities in between.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 79 — "He Rejoices Like a Mighty Man to Run the Course": the <i>Tzadik</i> Leaps Over All</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"He rejoices like a mighty man to run the course"</span> — the <span class="h">tzadik</span> runs this path swiftly. The moment a Jew leaves external wisdoms and bad thoughts (however much one manages to detach), the <span class="h">tzadik</span> immediately runs and repairs the entire path one should have traveled while estranged. He raises the person in the aspect of <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> — even without having purified the qualities (having corrupted greatly). He runs and leaps over everything: <span class="v">"the voice of my beloved — behold he comes, leaping over mountains, bounding over hills."</span> He raises one through <span class="h">Imma</span> lending garments to the daughter. The essential: some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — holding to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, exerting each time to flee bad thoughts. This too is exceedingly precious: <span class="v">"for Your sake we are killed all day"</span>; <span class="v">"when its waves surge, You still them."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 80 — Visiting <i>Tzadikim</i>'s Graves: the <i>Tzadik</i> Raises Even the Worst</div>

<div class="p">The greatness of visiting graves of great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. The <span class="h">tzadik</span> stood in battle in his life — breaking all evil inclinations, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> → <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> also ascended. At departure: ascending to the highest place — where there is no action or service to reveal <span class="h">Malchus</span>, only delighting in Hashem. But (as explained in <span class="lr">LM I:7</span>): the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s perfection is that even while above, he is also below. His desire: always to reveal <span class="h">Malchus</span> (Hashem's will, the purpose of Creation) — but this is only possible through service and deeds in this world. There, in his place, there is no deed. Therefore: his delight is when a lowly person from this world — even the worst — prostrates at his grave and requests <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and tikun. Immediately, through this arousal, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> does what he does — raising him in <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> — even without purified qualities (even corrupted ones). The <span class="h">tzadik</span> has this power through his immense exertion and self-sacrifice in life. As R' Shimon bar Yochai said: <span class="mx">"I can exempt the entire world..."</span> and <span class="mx">"if Yosam ben Uziyahu were with me, I could exempt even to the end..."</span> The essential: some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. Without any arousal at all: impossible — the entire Breaking was because there was no <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 81 — "You Said to Hashem: You Are My Master" — <i>Baisa Ila'ah</i> and <i>Tata'ah</i> Through the <i>Tzadikim</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You said to Hashem: You are my Master [<i>Adni</i>]."</span> <span class="h">Havayah</span> and <span class="h">Adnus</span> = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>. <span class="h">Havayah</span> = the Essential Name = <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> shining through <span class="h">Eheyeh</span> (= the contraction = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>). <span class="h">Adnus</span> = <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> = <span class="h">Malchus</span>. David said to his soul: it is fitting for you to say to <span class="h">Havayah</span> (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>): <span class="v">"You are <i>Adni</i>"</span> (= encompassing <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> with <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> = <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> = the essential perfection). <span class="v">"But my good is not upon You"</span> — I did not merit this through my good deeds; on the contrary, I corrupted greatly. <span class="v">"But to the holy ones who are in the earth — they are the mighty ones; all my desire is in them."</span> All my power for this — encompassing <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> with <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> (<span class="v">"You said to Hashem: <i>Adni</i>"</span>) — is only through the power of the holy <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who are in the earth. They can encompass both houses — even for one who never repaired the qualities, who corrupted greatly. They already did <span class="h">teshuvah</span> for all the world — we need only come to them so there will be some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>.</div>

<div class="cn">✦ End of Halachah 7 (§§ 1–81) ✦<br>The longest single halachah in Likutay Halachos<br>Based on <span class="lr">LM I:49</span>, <i>LaShemesh Ohel Bahem</i><br><br><i>Na Nach Nachma Nachman May'Uman</i></div>

